Subject: [PATCH] drm/msm/a6xx: Take an HFI response from the queue without its MSGQ bit

a6xx_hfi_wait_for_msg_interrupt() waits for the MSGQ bit of
GMU2HOST_INTR_INFO alone.  When the bit goes missing, a response that
is already in the response queue is not seen: the wait times out after
1 s, the caller gets -ETIMEDOUT (a6xx_gmu_set_freq() ignores it and
takes the new frequency as set), and the next message reports the
response as "Unexpected message id".  The wait holds gmu->lock and, for
a frequency change, the devfreq lock, which a submit takes when the GPU
leaves idle: the GPU gets no new work for that second, and hangcheck
then reports lockups that need no recovery.

Seen on two SM8550 (Adreno 740) tablets, up to about once an hour of
use.  Traces of three such GX_BW_PERF_VOTEs, each the first vote after
a GMU resume, found the response in the queue when the wait gave up
(read index 24, write index 28), with the vote's sequence number and no
error; in the one that recorded it, the queue was still empty when an
hfi interrupt ran 196 us into the wait.  One way the bit is lost is the
HFI interrupt handler, which clears it (addressed separately); whether
it is the only one is not known.

End the wait also when the response queue is not empty.  The poll
checks its condition once more after the timeout, so a response that
came in time is taken whatever happened to its bit.  The caller reads
the queue as before: a response with another sequence number is
reported and dropped, and an empty queue after a stale MSGQ bit still
waits again.  MSGQ is cleared as before; when the response is seen
before its bit, the bit stays set and ends the next wait early, which
then finds the queue empty and waits again.

The wait can now end on the queue's write index alone, a plain load
from the write-combined HFI memory, where the MMIO read of INTR_INFO
used to come first.  Order the reads of the message after the read of
the write index in a6xx_hfi_queue_read().

Fixes: df0dff132905 ("drm/msm/a6xx: Poll for HFI responses")

--- a/drivers/gpu/drm/msm/adreno/a6xx_hfi.c
+++ b/drivers/gpu/drm/msm/adreno/a6xx_hfi.c
@@ -39,6 +39,9 @@ static int a6xx_hfi_queue_read(struct a6
 		return 0;
 	}
 
+	/* Ensure the message is read only after the write index that covers it */
+	dma_rmb();
+
 	hdr = queue->data[index];
 
 	queue->history[(queue->history_idx++) % HFI_HISTORY_SZ] = index;
@@ -108,6 +111,14 @@ static int a6xx_hfi_queue_write(struct a
 	return 0;
 }
 
+/* Has the GMU queued a message that the host has not read yet? */
+static bool a6xx_hfi_queue_has_msg(struct a6xx_hfi_queue *queue)
+{
+	struct a6xx_hfi_queue_header *header = queue->header;
+
+	return header->read_index != READ_ONCE(header->write_index);
+}
+
 static int a6xx_hfi_wait_for_msg_interrupt(struct a6xx_gmu *gmu, u32 id, u32 seqnum)
 {
 	int ret;
@@ -116,9 +127,16 @@ static int a6xx_hfi_wait_for_msg_interru
 	struct a6xx_gpu *a6xx_gpu = container_of(gmu, struct a6xx_gpu, gmu);
 
 	do {
-		/* Wait for a response */
+		/*
+		 * Wait for a response.  The MSGQ bit announces it, but a
+		 * response that is already in the queue ends the wait too:
+		 * the bit can go missing while the response is there, and
+		 * the caller reads the queue either way.
+		 */
 		ret = gmu_poll_timeout(gmu, REG_A6XX_GMU_GMU2HOST_INTR_INFO, val,
-			val & A6XX_GMU_GMU2HOST_INTR_INFO_MSGQ, 100, 1000000);
+			(val & A6XX_GMU_GMU2HOST_INTR_INFO_MSGQ) ||
+			a6xx_hfi_queue_has_msg(&gmu->queues[HFI_RESPONSE_QUEUE]),
+			100, 1000000);
 
 		if (!ret)
 			break;
